 { Sergey Karjakin – Bu Xiangzhi, Russia 2008 The white king is quite exposed and Black needs to find the right way to include all of his pieces in the attack. This is done by making more out of the pawns, as is so often the case. } 33... d4 $1 { Instead, } ( 33... Qa3 34. Kd2 d4 35. Ke2 Qxc3 36. Qxf4 $13 { would allow the white king to escape to safety. } ) 
34. Kd2 $1 { White has no alternatives to this strong defensive move, as he would either lose a rook or be mated after } ( 34. cxd4 Qa3 $1 { Already this is not so easy to see when calculating in advance. } ) 
Qb2 35. Qxf4 $1 { The toughest defence, and again not easy to see. In the game, White collapsed and lost after } ( 35. Ke2 $2 Qxc2 36. Rd2 d3 $1 { when mate follows. } ) 
 ( 35. Qc7 Rc4 $1 36. Qxf4 { is no defence. Black wins according to his taste with either } Bd6 { Or } ( 36. Qxf4 Rxc3 37. Rc1 d3 $1 ) 
37. e5 Bxe5 $1 ) 
Bd6 $1 { ∓ This is the really difficult decision to make. Bu got it wrong in his annotations, even though he would have checked the game with a computer. Still I think it is entirely possible for a human to solve this. } ( 35... Ra2 $2 36. Rc1 Qxc3 37. Kd1 d3 38. Qd2 dxc2 39. Ke2 Qc7 { is given by Bu as winning, but White has a great resource here: } 40. Qd5 $1 Ra3 ( 40... Ra6 41. hxg7 Bxg7 42. Qxb5 $13 ) 
41. e5 $1 g6 42. e6 fxe6 43. Qxe6 Kh8 44. Qf6 { with a draw. } ) 
36. e5 { The alternative is: } ( 36. Qxd6 Qxc3 37. Ke2 Qe3 38. Kf1 Qxf3 39. Ke1 Qxh1 40. Kf2 Qh4 41. Qg3 Qxg3 42. Kxg3 Rc4 43. Ra1 gxh6 44. gxh6 Kf8 45. Ra2 Ke7 { Black has a pawn more in the rook ending with serious winning chances. } ) 
dxc3+ 37. Ke1 Rxf4 38. exd6 Qxc2 39. d7 Qxd1+ 40. Kxd1 Rd4+ 41. Kc2 Rxd7 42. Kxc3 Rd5 $17 43. Ra1 gxh6 44. gxh6 f6 45. Ra7 Rh5 46. Kd4 Rxh6 47. f4 { Can White hold this ending? It looks difficult. Yes, this was meant to be one of the easier positions! As you can probably guess, I would expect you to take anywhere from thirty minutes to an hour-and-a-half trying to make sense of these positions. If things go as planned and your abilities improve along the way, it is my hope that you will be able to solve the ridiculously difficult positions at the end, not a lot slower than the “easier” positions in the beginning. } 
