 { But first of all, you are probably curious to find out whether or not Black should have thrown in 17...e5 before taking on d3 in the introductory position. Before we can understand the difference, we need to work out the main line. Manuel Bosboom – Daniel Fridman, Gibraltar 2012 It makes sense to calculate this position only once and then throw in ...e5 and Bxe5 whenever we like. The main line is: } ( 17. f4 exd3 18. Nf6 Bxf6 19. gxf6 g6 20. Qh6 Bf3 21. Be2 dxe2 22. Kc2 { Forget the fact that you need to understand quite a lot of things before you can get here; that is all part of the challenge - or torture if you like. } ) 
 { Let us instead look at the position with and without the insertion of the moves ...e6-e5 and Bxe5: I am sure that even looking at these two positions like this, rather than trying to make sense of them in your head, will offer you a challenge. But there you go – spot the difference! In the game Fridman played: } e5 18. Bxe5 { The fact that } ( 18. Nh6 $6 { draws after } gxh6 $6 { Black has a stronger possibility in } ( 18. Bxe5 Kh8 $1 19. Nxf7 Rxf7 20. g6 ( 20. Qxf7 $2 Qd6 { with full control. } ) 
h6 21. gxf7 Bf6 { gives Black the advantage. For example: } 22. Rg6 Qf8 23. Bxe5 Bxe5 24. Re6 Bc8 25. Rxe5 Bd7 26. Bg2 Nc5 { and White is struggling to make his compensation count. } ) 
19. Bxe5 hxg5 20. Qh6 f6 21. Rxg5 Kf7 22. Rg7 Ke8 23. Qh5 Kd7 24. Qf5 Kc6 25. Bg3 Bc8 26. Qb5 Kb7 27. Rxe7 Qxe7 28. Qd5 { is both “not human” and irrelevant. } ) 
exd3 { Now there are no choices. } ( 18... Bxg5 $2 19. Nh6 $1 Kh8 20. Nxf7 Rxf7 21. Qxf7 Qf8 22. Qxb7 $18 ) 
19. Nf6+ ( 19. Qh6 $2 Bf3 20. Be2 Bxe2 21. Ke1 Bb4 22. Bc3 Bxc3 ) 
Bxf6 20. gxf6 g6 21. Rg3 $4 { Nothing less than an outright blunder. No doubt Bosboom was convinced he was winning due to the prevention of the bishop check, but he soon got a cold shower. The correct move was therefore the only sensible alternative: } ( 21. Qh6 $1 { Now the game should end in perpetual check. } Bf3 22. Be2 dxe2 ( 22. Rg3 Bxe2 $2 23. Ke1 { is just winning for White. } ) 
23. Kc2 Be4 $2 { Here Black can and must force a draw with } ( 23. Rg3 Qd2 24. Kxb3 Qd1 { The other attempt even loses. } ) 
24. Kb2 $3 ( 24. Kxb3 { is good enough for a draw, but the White king is dancing away. } ) 
Qd2 25. Ka3 Qa5 { Finally it is time to take the knight, and now it is a pleasure as well. } 26. Kxb3 { Black has run out of sensible checks and will have to assist the white king in going in the opposite direction. } Bc2 27. Kxc2 Qa2 28. Kd3 Rad8 29. Ke4 $1 Qc2 30. Kf3 Qf5 31. Kg2 $18 { There are no sensible checks left. } ) 
Bf3+ $1 { A nice but rather simple tactical blow, ruining White’s coordination. Now Black comes first with his counterattack. } 22. Qxf3 Qd7 23. Qf4 ( 23. Qh5 Nc5 24. Qh6 Qa4 { and Black wins. } ) 
Nc5 24. Bh3 Qa4+ 25. Ke1 d2+ 26. Kxd2 Qa2+ 27. Kd1 Rad8+ 28. Bd4 Rfe8 29. Bf5 Nb3 
 0-1

