 { By this point it is probably no surprise to you that the correct choice was: } 17... exd3 $1 { Black refuses to give White a chance to vacate the b2-square for his king. Compared with the note to White’s 21st move in the main game, this one detail makes the difference between winning and losing (or at least having to settle for a draw) . } 18. Nf6+ Bxf6 19. gxf6 g6 20. Qh6 Bf3+ 21. Be2 $1 { As forced as ever. } dxe2+ 22. Kc2 { This time around the white king does not have the b2-square available, and he is brutally hunted down on the light squares. } Be4+ $1 23. Kxb3 Qd3+ 24. Ka2 ( 24. Bc3 Qc2 $19 ) 
Qxc4+ 25. Ka3 Qd3+ 26. Ka2 Bd5+ 27. Ka1 Qd1+ 28. Bc1 Qa4+ 29. Kb2 Qb3+ 30. Ka1 Qa2# { Determination is the only thing that can carry you through the next 75 positions. But at the end of it you will be a changed man and ready for anything. You might even have improved your calculation! } 
